{
  "format": "hoglake-bounds-vectors",
  "version": 1,
  "description": "Cross-language differential vectors for the Iceberg single-value binary bounds codec. Each vector is (type, type_params, value, hex): hex is the lowercase hex of the exact encoding. Any implementation (Python, JVM, ...) must satisfy encode(value) == unhex(hex) and, unless verify == 'encode_only', decode(unhex(hex)) == value and encode(decode(unhex(hex))) == unhex(hex). Vectors additionally carry `wire` or `wire_refused` - the read-time JSON rendering contract - per wire_conventions below.",
  "value_conventions": {
    "boolean": "'true' | 'false'",
    "int8": "decimal integer string",
    "int16": "decimal integer string",
    "int": "decimal integer string (int32 domain)",
    "long": "decimal integer string (int64 domain)",
    "uint8": "decimal integer string",
    "uint16": "decimal integer string",
    "uint32": "decimal integer string in [0, 2^32)",
    "uint64": "decimal integer string in [0, 2^64)",
    "float": "decimal string or 'NaN'/'Infinity'/'-Infinity'; for NaN the hex bytes are authoritative (payload NaNs share the 'NaN' value token)",
    "double": "same conventions as float, 8-byte width",
    "decimal": "UNSCALED integer as decimal string; type_params.scale applies",
    "date": "days since 1970-01-01, decimal integer string",
    "time": "microseconds since midnight, decimal integer string",
    "timestamp_s": "MICROSECONDS since 1970-01-01T00:00:00, decimal integer string (the declared seconds precision is metadata; bounds are micros)",
    "timestamp_ms": "MICROSECONDS since 1970-01-01T00:00:00, decimal integer string (the declared millis precision is metadata; bounds are micros)",
    "timestamp": "microseconds since 1970-01-01T00:00:00, decimal integer string",
    "timestamp_ns": "NANOSECONDS since 1970-01-01T00:00:00, decimal integer string",
    "timestamptz": "microseconds since 1970-01-01T00:00:00Z, decimal integer string",
    "string": "the string itself (JSON string escapes apply)",
    "json": "the document text itself (JSON string escapes apply)",
    "uuid": "canonical 8-4-4-4-12 lowercase form",
    "binary": "standard base64 of the raw bytes"
  },
  "wire_conventions": "Additive JSON-wire pinning for the read-time decode surface (GET .../files/{fileId}/stats and POST /v1/debug/decode-bound; conventions documented in openapi/hoglake.yaml FileColumnStats). `wire` is the decoded bound as it appears in a JSON response: booleans as booleans; every integer type as an exact JSON number; decimal as an exact JSON number at the column's scale in PLAIN notation (unscaled x 10^-scale with exactly `scale` fraction digits - '1.50', never 1E-38); float/double as shortest-round-trip JSON numbers with the string sentinels \"Infinity\"/\"-Infinity\" (JVM-side tests compare these by VALUE - exponent spelling differs per language; the stored tokens are the Python oracle's); date/time/timestamps as ISO-8601 strings whose trailing zero units are ELIDED ('00:00', '1970-01-01T00:00' - seconds omitted when seconds and fraction are both zero; nonzero fractions print in groups of 3 digits; years above 9999 use the expanded '+YYYYY' form), timestamptz as a UTC instant with a trailing Z and seconds never elided; string/json/uuid as strings; binary as base64. `wire_refused` marks a value the JSON layer refuses instead of rendering (NaN bounds - the store never holds one, and the decode endpoint answers a named 422). bound_normalization cases additionally carry raw_wire/stored_wire: the rendering of raw_hex and stored_hex, pinning that a stored -0.0 renders with its sign. Every wire cell is generated and checked by tests/wire_oracle.py (independent of the JVM implementation); regenerate with `uv run python tests/wire_oracle.py --write`, and treat any --check disagreement as a cross-language finding, not a cell to edit.",
